    "result_text": "The trim rule now applies to the sheet's settled-total, open-total and cut total, and the consolidated figures have the three new trimmed totals. `bin/e2e` passes all 4 acceptance tests.\n\nThe change is front end only. The server doesn't compute these totals, and the spec expects no new audit records.\n\n- **Settled-total and open-total** (`features/rates/split.slot.tsx`): these now use the existing `trimmedSum`, which drops the single highest and lowest person's figure when three or more people have one. They used to be a plain sum.\n- **Cut total** (`features/pools/cuttotal.slot.tsx`): this used to add up every cut directly. It now works out each person's cut total and then trims over those.\n- **Consolidated figures** (`features/consolidated/consolidated.slot.tsx`): each sheet's figures now also carry every person's settled-total, open-total and cut total. The new `consolidated-settled-total`, `consolidated-open-total` and `consolidated-cut-total` are each trimmed once over all the people in the tree. This works the same way as the existing consolidated due and overdue totals.\n\nA person who belongs to no pool counts as a cut of 0.00 in the trim. So does someone on a child sheet with no pools. That's what the spec expects: the tree's cut figures 0, 0, 3, 3, 4 give 6.00.\n\nI left the per-person figures and the dashboard alone, since the request didn't cover them.",
